    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Background\r\nCardiovascular disease claims more lives each year than all forms of cancer combined, and atherosclerosis, a form of cardiovascular disease, is the process of fatty deposit build-up in arterial cells to form atherosclerotic lesions. Recent studies show that vascular smooth muscle cells (SMCs) act as a cardiovascular risk factor independent of cholesterol levels, yet not much is known because SMCs tend to lose their lineage-specific markers during plaque progression, which results in the underestimation of SMC contribution to plaque expansion using traditional histology methods. For the same reason, little is known about SMC biology in atherosclerosis and in developing therapeutics to treat diseased SMCs in the plaques.\r\nMethods and Results\r\nTo overcome this problem, a unique SMC lineage-tracing murine model carrying a \"Rainbow\" reporter was developed. When exposed to tamoxifen, this mouse underwent gene recombination and randomly expressed one of three colors - yellow, blue or red - exclusively in SMCs such that each cell and its descendants expressed the same color. Mice containing the \"Rainbow\" reporter were crossed with Apolipoprotein E knockout (ApoE-/-) mice, a murine model of atherosclerosis, to generate \"Rainbow mice\" for studying SMCs during atherosclerosis. Use of this model showed that SMCs underwent clonal expansion in the plaque. Flow cytometry analysis subsequently showed that diseased SMCs in the plaque from Rainbow mice expressed higher levels of the \"don't eat me\" molecule CD47 compared to that of healthy SMCs.  SMCs from the plaque were also sorted to feed the phagocytes, and CD47high SMCs were found to be more resistant to efferocytosis. Then, Rainbow mice were fed with 18 weeks of high fat diet to induce atherosclerosis and were treated with control IgG or anti-CD47 antibody. The anti-CD47 antibody treated mice showed significantly reduced plaque size and SMC clonal expansion. \r\nConclusion \r\nCD47 overexpression facilitated SMCs clonal expansion by allowing them to escape from efferocytosis. Ultimately, the use of an anti-CD47 strategy was potentially beneficial to help remove diseased SMCs from the plaque to decrease the size of the established plaque and lower the risk of heart attack and stroke.\r\n ",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
